The simple fact is that NetHack is what the Dev Team does with it; it's 
_their_ program, not ours.  As many folks have said, if that makes you 
unhappy you are welcome to start your own development effort (and the 
Dev Team has even been nice enough to give you the option of forking 
their sources to get you started) but that is quite simply your only 
option.  They've given you the software to play with and even the right 
to change it, stick a new name on it and start passing it out to anyone 
who's interested and that's already way more than most software 
producers do.

It's _very_ clear at this point that the Dev Team is not going to change 
their style to suit you (which is fine by me, because frankly I think 
the style is perfectly appropriate for the game).  It really is time to 
set aside the complaints and decide what you should do about the fact 
that they have no obligation to be what you want, and apparently have no 
inclination to be so either.

You clearly have a style in mind for how you think a development team 
should behave, and probably some ideas about changes you'd like to see 
in the game, so recruiting folks of like mind is probably the place to 
start.  I'm guessing that rgrn would be more welcoming to some 
recruiting posts than it's been to the complaints.

I should point out that I'm not out to rain on your enthusiasm; I just 
think that you should point that enthusiasm in a useful direction.  The 
/dev/null/nethack tournament would not exist at all if we'd spent our 
time trying to get the Dev Team to hold one rather than taking what they 
had produced and building something new out of it ourselves;  NAO 
wouldn't exist if they'd spent their time trying to get the Dev Team to 
run a game hosting system rather than building one themselves.

The Dev Team is what it is, and they work the way they choose; if you 
want something else, you really are going to have to take responsibility 
for building it.

-robin


